Title:
Hunting the Watchers: Detecting and Enumerating EDR User-Mode Hooks

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post explains how Windows EDR agents gain user-mode visibility by injecting DLLs and inline-hooking Native API exports (primarily in `ntdll.dll`) by overwriting syscall stub prologues with jump-based patches. It provides a practical methodology to enumerate which `Nt*`/`Zw*` functions are hooked by comparing in-memory function prologues against a clean on-disk baseline of the same DLL. The author details common x64 inline hook patterns (relative JMP, MOV+JMP, PUSH+RET, indirect JMP, INT3) and how to classify them by opcode signatures. Two automated scanners are presented: a C++ implementation (simple RVA assumptions) and a more robust Go implementation that correctly translates RVA-to-file-offset via section headers and reduces false positives by filtering non-code exports and intra-module JMPs. The tooling can also resolve hook jump targets to identify the responsible EDR module/DLL. It’s useful for red team reconnaissance and bypass planning, as well as blue team integrity monitoring and coverage assessment of EDR user-mode telemetry.

Technical Focus:
- Windows user-mode inline hooking of `ntdll.dll` syscall stubs
- Syscall stub byte-pattern baselining and divergence detection
- PE export table parsing (EAT) and RVA resolution (disk vs memory)
- Hook opcode classification (E9 rel32, FF25, 48B8+FFE0, PUSH/RET, CC)
- Jump target resolution and module attribution (`GetModuleHandleEx`, `GetModuleFileName`)
- False-positive reduction (executable-section filtering, forwarded exports, intra-module JMP detection)

Use Cases:
- Enumerate EDR-hooked `Nt*` APIs to infer monitored behaviors and blind spots
- Identify which vendor DLL is performing hooks by resolving JMP targets
- Validate EDR deployment integrity (detect unhooking/tampering in suspicious processes)
- Build a hook coverage matrix for detection engineering and purple-team exercises
- Compare hook coverage across EDR versions/updates for regression tracking

Keywords:
Windows, EDR, user-mode hooks, ntdll.dll, inline hooking, syscall stub, Native API, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtProtectVirtualMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory, NtCreateThreadEx, PE format, Export Address Table, RVA, trampoline, JMP rel32, INT3, ReadProcessMemory, GetModuleHandleEx, MITRE ATT&CK T1055